---
// suppliers.astro — the supplier-facing guide: how a manufacturer or
// distributor actually gets from "the government buys this" to "we won the
// award." General, accurate, no invented thresholds or legal advice — this
// page has to be trustworthy on its own, not just a funnel to /open/.
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SignupForm from '../components/SignupForm.astro';
import { SITE_URL } from '../consts';

const title = 'How to Sell Parts to the Government (DIBBS Guide)';
const description =
  'How to sell parts to the government: SAM.gov registration, your CAGE code, how to bid on DIBBS, and where to find open DLA solicitations. A free plain guide.';
const canonicalUrl = `${SITE_URL}/suppliers/`;

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home', url: '/' },
  { name: 'For Suppliers', url: canonicalUrl },
];

const faq = [
  {
    question: 'How do I bid on DIBBS?',
    answer:
      'Register in SAM.gov, get your CAGE code, then create a DIBBS account at dibbs.bsm.dla.mil. Open an RFQ, read its requirements and submit your quote through DIBBS before the return-by date.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Do I need a CAGE code to sell parts to the government?',
    answer:
      'Yes for federal work. The CAGE code is issued during SAM.gov registration, which is free, and it identifies your company on every solicitation and award.',
  },
  {
    question: 'How do I become a DLA supplier?',
    answer:
      'There is no separate approval to become a DLA supplier for competitive parts. Once you are registered in SAM.gov you can quote any open solicitation whose specification you can meet.',
  },
  {
    question: 'Where do I find open DLA solicitations?',
    answer:
      'DIBBS lists them, and NSN Explorer indexes them by federal supply class with price history, then links back to DIBBS to bid. Both are free.',
  },
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  canonicalUrl={canonicalUrl}
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs}
  faq={faq}
>
  <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <a href="/">Home</a> › For Suppliers
  </nav>

  <p class="eyebrow">For Suppliers</p>
  <h1>How to sell parts to the government</h1>
  <p class="lede">How to win federal parts work</p>
  <p class="lede">
    This guide explains how to sell parts to the government as a manufacturer or distributor: what
    a national stock number is, what SAM.gov registration and a CAGE code get you, and how to bid
    on DIBBS, the DLA board where most parts solicitations are posted. It is free, it is not legal
    advice, and it links to the official systems at every step. Open solicitations on NSN Explorer
    come from DIBBS and are refreshed every day.
  </p>

  <hr class="rule" />

  <section>
    <h2>The basics</h2>
    <p class="gloss">
      A handful of terms cover most of what you'll see on a solicitation. None of this is legal
      advice — always read the actual solicitation for its specific requirements.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>NSN (National Stock Number)</strong> — the 13-digit code that identifies a specific
        part across the entire federal supply system. Every solicitation, award, and price on this
        site is organized by NSN.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>FSC (Federal Supply Class)</strong> — the 4-digit category a part belongs to (the
        first four digits of its NSN), grouping similar items like o-rings, bearings, or fasteners.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Solicitation / RFQ (request for quote)</strong> — the government's public notice
        that it wants to buy a quantity of a part by a certain date. Suppliers respond with a
        price and delivery quote.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Award</strong> — the solicitation's outcome: which supplier won, at what price and
        quantity.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>CAGE code</strong> — a five-character ID assigned to a company that does business
        with the U.S. government, used to identify suppliers on solicitations and awards.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>AMSC / competition</strong> — the Acquisition Method Suffix Code, which signals
        whether a part is open to any qualified supplier or restricted to sources the government
        has already approved (sole-source). Open parts are generally the easier ones for a new
        supplier to break into.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Set-aside</strong> — a solicitation reserved for a category of business, such as a
        small business or a socio-economic program, rather than open to all bidders.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <hr class="rule" />

  <section>
    <h2>What you need to bid</h2>
    <p class="gloss">
      In general terms — the specifics depend on the item and the buying agency, so treat this as
      an orientation, not a checklist to submit on:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        A free registration in{' '}
        <a href="https://sam.gov" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener">SAM.gov</a>, the
        government's system of record for contractors, which issues your Unique Entity ID (UEI)
        and is where your CAGE code gets tied to your business.
      </li>
      <li>
        The ability to actually meet the part's specification and any quality requirements the
        solicitation calls out — drawings, material certs, inspection standards, and so on.
      </li>
      <li>
        A quote submitted through the solicitation's own channel, before its closing date. Most
        Defense Logistics Agency (DLA) solicitations are posted and bid through DLA's DIBBS bid
        board at{' '}
        <a href="https://dibbs.bsm.dla.mil" target="_blank" rel="nofollow noopener">dibbs.bsm.dla.mil</a>.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <hr class="rule" />

  <section>
    <h2>How to use NSN Explorer to find and win work</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <strong>Browse open demand by your category.</strong> Start at{' '}
        <a href="/open/">Open Demand</a> and drill into the Federal Supply Classes you can make or
        source.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Filter to what you can actually win.</strong> Narrow by closing-soon, open
        competition (rather than sole-source), and the quantities you can supply.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Open a part to see the specs and price history.</strong> Every NSN page shows past
        purchases and prices, so you can quote with a sense of where the market has actually
        cleared — not a guess.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Click through to the official record to bid.</strong> Every solicitation links
        straight to its DIBBS record, where you submit your actual quote.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Sign up for alerts so you don't miss the next one.</strong> Tell us your categories
        and we'll email you when a new RFQ opens.
      </li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <hr class="rule" />

  <section class="faq">
    <h2 class="section-title">Common questions about selling to the government</h2>
    {faq.map((f) => (
      <>
        <h3>{f.question}</h3>
        <p class="gloss">{f.answer}</p>
      </>
    ))}
  </section>

  <hr class="rule" />

  <div class="note">
    <b>The honest part.</b> NSN Explorer is an independent index of public government data. We are
    not affiliated with the U.S. government, we don't submit bids on your behalf, and we don't
    broker or sell parts. We sell nothing and offer no recommendations — every figure here links to
    the official record behind it, so you can verify it and bid through the real channel yourself.
  </div>

  <div class="btns">
    <a class="btn" href="/open/">Browse open demand →</a>
  </div>

  <SignupForm
    hook="shop_alerts"
    title="Get new RFQs in your categories, by email"
    description="Tell us the NSNs or supply classes you can make or source. We'll email you when the government opens a solicitation you can bid — in time to quote it, before it closes."
    cta="Send me open demand"
  />
</BaseLayout>
